1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:04,497
Before you run this agent even once, do the arithmetic in your head.

2
00:00:04,497 --> 00:00:05,656
Four open renewals.

3
00:00:05,656 --> 00:00:08,278
A total premium you can add up on one hand.

4
00:00:08,278 --> 00:00:12,304
One renewal, Dunmore Electrical, close enough to its date to flag.

5
00:00:12,304 --> 00:00:13,767
That is your answer key.

6
00:00:13,767 --> 00:00:18,890
Write the key down before the first run, not after, for the same anchoring reason as

7
00:00:18,890 --> 00:00:24,013
Section 4's count-first rule, sharpened by the fact that these are numbers now: once

8
00:00:24,013 --> 00:00:29,075
the agent's total is on screen, your own addition quietly checks itself against the

9
00:00:29,075 --> 00:00:30,722
agent instead of the sheet.

10
00:00:30,722 --> 00:00:35,540
The whole key takes under a minute of mental arithmetic, and that minute is the

11
00:00:35,540 --> 00:00:37,736
trial's entire measuring instrument.

12
00:00:37,736 --> 00:00:41,517
An answer key you wrote after seeing the answers is not a key.

13
00:00:41,517 --> 00:00:42,371
It is an echo.

14
00:00:42,921 --> 00:00:45,487
Run it against the pinned data and compare.

15
00:00:45,487 --> 00:00:50,500
If the numbers match, the wiring and the judgement are both sound, for this snapshot

16
00:00:50,500 --> 00:00:51,275
of the sheet.

17
00:00:51,275 --> 00:00:56,228
That phrase, "for this snapshot," is doing quiet work, and it is worth sitting with

18
00:00:56,228 --> 00:00:57,004
for a second.

19
00:00:57,004 --> 00:01:01,838
Know what a near miss looks like here, because it is subtler than a wrong answer.

20
00:01:01,838 --> 00:01:06,791
The dangerous report is the one where the counts are right, the total is right, and

21
00:01:06,791 --> 00:01:11,506
one detail is quietly off: the correct number of at-risk renewals but the wrong

22
00:01:11,506 --> 00:01:16,339
client named, or a fluent paragraph in which one figure came from nowhere you can

23
00:01:16,339 --> 00:01:16,876
point to.

24
00:01:16,876 --> 00:01:21,710
Fluency is not accuracy, and on a numbers report the check is always the numbers,

25
00:01:21,710 --> 00:01:22,665
never the prose.

26
00:01:22,665 --> 00:01:27,618
If any single line cannot be traced to a cell in the pinned data, that line failed,

27
00:01:27,618 --> 00:01:31,079
and the report fails with it, however well the rest reads.

28
00:01:31,629 --> 00:01:36,758
This pipeline, these five rows, this exact Brightwell line, comes back later in this

29
00:01:36,758 --> 00:01:37,186
course.

30
00:01:37,186 --> 00:01:42,254
Not because it changes here, but because somewhere down the line, someone edits the

31
00:01:42,254 --> 00:01:47,321
sheet the agent reads from, and the agent has no way to know that changed anything.

32
00:01:47,321 --> 00:01:48,420
Hold that thought.

33
00:01:48,420 --> 00:01:50,252
Section 7 is where it matters.

34
00:01:50,252 --> 00:01:54,587
The unsettling part is that nothing needs to go wrong for this to bite.

35
00:01:54,587 --> 00:01:59,472
A shared sheet is a living thing: a colleague sorts it to find their own client,

36
00:01:59,472 --> 00:02:04,540
someone inserts a row to log a new account, a Status value gets typed in lower case

37
00:02:04,540 --> 00:02:05,883
by someone in a hurry.

38
00:02:05,883 --> 00:02:10,829
Every one of those is normal, correct use of a spreadsheet, and every one changes

39
00:02:10,829 --> 00:02:14,370
what the agent reads without anyone having made a mistake.

40
00:02:14,370 --> 00:02:19,377
Your pinned test can never cover the ways other people will use a sheet you share.

41
00:02:19,377 --> 00:02:22,003
That is not a reason to distrust the build.

42
00:02:22,003 --> 00:02:27,132
It is the reason the trial runs on the living sheet, for three Mondays, against your

43
00:02:27,132 --> 00:02:29,452
hand count, before trust gets granted.

44
00:02:30,002 --> 00:02:34,838
With the wiring proven, next lecture connects this agent to a real sheet and ships

45
00:02:34,838 --> 00:02:35,015
it.
